{
  "genes": [
    {
      "type": "formulation",
      "text": "Replace the smoothed maximum with an epigraph variable t and constraints (f*f)[k] <= t, fixing sum(f)=1. Optimize log(Q)-log(t), where Q=(2 sum(c²)+sum(c[k]c[k+1]))/3. Use sequential linearized subproblems with nonnegativity bounds and a trust region; accept steps only after exact rescoring."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Use constraint generation for the epigraph formulation. Start with representative near-maximal convolution lags, solve the restricted step, then compute the full convolution and add violated or newly near-active lags. Retain previously binding constraints until several accepted iterations show substantial slack."
    },
    {
      "type": "formulation",
      "text": "Parameterize the incumbent by multiplicative weights on contiguous nonzero runs, preserving each run's internal profile. Split long runs into segments of at most 256 cells. Optimize these weights under fixed total mass before attempting cellwise polishing, retaining the original candidate as a fallback."
    },
    {
      "type": "numeric",
      "text": "Estimate nonnegative multipliers on near-maximal convolution lags by minimizing the stationarity residual of the exact log-score, with multipliers summing to one. Use the resulting reduced gradient to rank support births and weight transfers, rather than selecting moves from a single maximizing lag."
    },
    {
      "type": "formulation",
      "text": "Optimize proposed support births directly in nonnegative amplitudes rather than squared coordinates, whose derivatives vanish at zero. Add batches of zero cells ranked by reduced gradient, fund their mass from existing support, and jointly optimize birth amplitudes and donor weights inside a small trust region."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Test coordinated topology changes involving 4–32 nonzero runs simultaneously. Translate selected runs by small integer offsets while preserving their values and resolving collisions by addition. Bias selections toward weakly constrained runs, then jointly refit affected run weights and accept only exact-score improvements."
    },
    {
      "type": "numeric",
      "text": "Evaluate every proposed search direction using a geometric grid of step sizes and the exact nonsmoothed score. Include steps much smaller than the optimizer's proposed step, and refine around the best bracket. This lets useful directions survive when a flat convolution plateau makes conventional smooth line searches unreliable."
    },
    {
      "type": "numeric",
      "text": "Choose LogSumExp sharpness adaptively rather than by a fixed schedule. Normalize the convolution maximum to one, measure log(sum(exp(beta*(c-max(c)))))/beta, and increase beta until this excess is below a specified tolerance between 1e-8 and 1e-6. Reassess sharpness after substantial accepted moves."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Checkpoint exact scores throughout optimization, not only at stage boundaries. Rescore accepted iterates at least every five iterations, preserve the best exact candidate independently of optimizer state, and restart from that checkpoint after sustained deterioration. Allow continuation trajectories to deteriorate temporarily without losing intermediate improvements."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Explore mass redistribution between large spatial regions while preserving microscopic support. Partition the grid into 16–128 contiguous regions, multiply each region's existing values by an independent positive weight, and optimize the weights jointly. Repeat with shifted partition boundaries before returning to run-level refinement."
    }
  ]
}
